{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n31 \n \n \nAppendix B. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS \nIncrements 59 and 60 \n17 December 2019 \n30-day Operational Accomplishments Report  \n\u2026 for the International Space Station (ISS) Increments 59 and 60 \n \nACME \nAdvanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments \nFlame Design \n \nFlame Design is one of several independent experiments that are part of the ACME project, which \nuses a single modular set of hardware to carry out studies of flames burning gaseous fuels in the \nISS\u2019 Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR).  The investigation will be carried out in two rounds of tests, \nwhere the first occurred in Increments 59 and 60.  It is currently expected that the second round \nof tests will be conducted during Increment 63. \n \nPrepared by the PI team and Dennis Stocker (dennis.p.stocker@nasa.gov), the ACME Project \nScientist, who is the primary contact for E-FIELD Flames and ACME information. \n \n \n \nPrincipal Investigator (PI): \nProf. Richard L. Axelbaum, Washington University in St. Louis, MO \nCo-Investigator (Co-I): \nProf. Peter B. Sunderland, University of Maryland, College Park, MD \nCo-Investigator (Co-I): \nDr. David L. Urban, NASA Glenn Research Center, Cleveland, OH \nLead collaborator: \nDr. Sergei Frolov, N.N. Semenov Institute of Chemical Physics, \nRussian Academy of Sciences, Moscow, Russia \nNASA Project Scientist (PS): Dennis P. Stocker, NASA Glenn Research Center, Cleveland, OH \nPS team members: \nDr. Uday G. Hegde, Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, \nOH \n \nDr. Vedha Nayagam, Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, \nOH \n \n \n \nI.  Introduction \nThe Flame Design experiment is conducted in the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on the \nInternational Space Station, as part of the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments \n(ACME) project.  The primary goal of the Flame Design experiment is to improve the \nunderstanding of soot inception and control, in order to enable the optimization of oxygen \nenriched combustion and the \u2018design\u2019 of non-premixed flames that are both robust and soot free.  \nAn outside review panel declared that Flame Design \u201c\u2026 could lead to greatly improved burner \ndesigns that are efficient and less polluting than current designs.\"", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 31}}